#text(weight: "bold")[
  The responses of cells in the primate hippocampus during navigation have received increased focus in recent years. Unlike the responses of the neurons in the rodent hippocampus, which mainly appears to encode allocentric spatial coordinates, primate hippocampus cells exhibit a mix of both spatial and view related responses. This mix has been linked to the greater importance of vision during navigation in these species. Indeed, evidence now suggests that this reliance on vision creates a “viewpoint” coding of space, where specific visual scenes are stitched together using path integration mechanisms to form an egocentric navigation code. Despite these recent advances, the full range of navigation related inputs encoded by primate hippocampal cells is still being actively explored. In this study, we recorded the activity of hippocampal cells as a non-human primate subject navigated a virtual reality maze. We found that 48.6% of the cells encoded virtual position in the maze while 52.7% encoded virtual gaze position. Supporting the viewpoint coding hypothesis, we found that 43.4% of the cells encoded both, and that a substantial fraction (23.4%) of these exhibited conjunctions consisted with viewpoint coding.

/*The 23.4% come from 37 cells that are both conjunctive and landmark, out of 158 cells that are both place and view selective*/


]

= Introduction
Across multiple mammalian species, the hippocampus and its neighboring structures in the hippocampal formation are important for the formation of long-term memories, including spatial memory (#cite(<eichenbaumCanWeReconcile2014>, form:"prose"), #cite(<hartleySpaceBrainHow2014>, form: "prose"), #cite(<schillerMemorySpaceUnderstanding2015>, form: "prose"), #cite(<lismanViewpointsHowHippocampus2017>, form:"prose")). Importantly, in humans, the hippocampus is a major, and one of the first, brain areas to atrophy in dementia #cite(<piniBrainAtrophyAlzheimers2016>), and the disorientation resulting from deteriorated spatial cognition is incapacitating and an enormous cost to public health. Although the neural code supporting spatial memory has been well-studied in rodents, much less is known in humans. Non-human primates are an invaluable model for understanding the neural basis of normative spatial cognition in humans, and will allow for the development of targeted behavioral interventions for dementia patients. 

Spatial memory has been most extensively studied in rodents. Critical features of occupied space are represented on the single-cell level, with place cells selectively representing location, head direction cells representing heading, border cells signaling environmental boundaries, and grid cells possibly signaling distance travelled #cite(<okeefeHippocampusSpatialMap1971>); #cite(<okeefeHippocampusCognitiveMap1978>);#cite(<taubeHeaddirectionCellsRecorded1990>);#cite(<fyhnSpatialRepresentationEntorhinal2004>);#cite(<haftingMicrostructureSpatialMap2005>);#cite(<moserPlaceCellsGrid2008>);#cite(<solstadRepresentationGeometricBorders2008>);#cite(<leverBoundaryVectorCells2009>);#cite(<buzsakiMemoryNavigationTheta2013>);#cite(<rowlandTenYearsGrid2016>). Similar neural codes have been found in bats #cite(<yartsevRepresentationThreeDimensionalSpace2013>);#cite(<finkelsteinThreedimensionalHeaddirectionCoding2015>) and birds #cite(<payneNeuralRepresentationsSpace2021>) #cite(<payneRemoteActivationPlace2024>), but much less is known in primates, both non-human and human. Early studies in behaving non-human primates (NHPs) reported mixed results for place cells, with some studies finding limited place responses in chaired animals or animals moving along a linear track #cite(<onoHippocampusSpaceAre1991>);#cite(<nishijoRelationshipMonkeyHippocampus1997>), while others found no place cells in the hippocampus #cite(<rollsSpatialViewCells1997>);#cite(<rollsSpatialViewCells1999>). More recently, using largely-linear virtual reality mazes as a proxy for real-world navigation, one group found place cells at a much lower prevalence compared to rodents #cite(<gulliContextdependentRepresentationsObjects2020>), while another group found cells simultaneously sensitive to place and direction #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>). To date, only a few studies in NHPs have found place cells in an open field environment #cite(<ludvigDetectingLocationspecificNeuronal2004>) #cite(<maoSpatialModulationHippocampal2021>) #cite(<yanDivergentNeuralRepresentations2026>), and another found head direction cells in a chaired NHP #cite(<robertsonHeadDirectionCells1999>), but neither of these studies have undertaken the extensive characterization seen in rodent studies of the dependence of these cells on sensory cues, or the coherence of the ensembles. As yet, there is still no evidence of grid cells (but see #cite(<killianMapVisualSpace2012>) for some evidence in a task that did not include navigation), speed cells, or border cells in the NHP. That so few examples of spatial neurons have been found in the NHP is surprising, given that many species of non-human primates also depend on foraging for survival, and their range may be much larger than that of the common rat  #cite(<salmiSpatialCognitionWestern2020>). The use of linear track mazes may be part of the reason for this. While allowing for rapid and uniform sampling of the environment, the limitations of one-dimensional linear track mazes are many, including the inability to dissociate spatial variables that are forced by the setup to covary (e.g. place and head direction), the lack of open space in which grid and border responses may be properly assessed, and stereotyped movements and velocities. 

