/*Beyond cross-species similarities in the basic neural code for occupied space, differences in spatial cognition between primates and rodents are expected. One important divergence involves low-frequency local field potentials during navigation. In rodents, theta waves (7 to 9 Hz) in the hippocampus are a continuous feature during navigation (REF). Theta frequency varies with movement speed, and place cell activity is entrained to the theta cycle in a signature phase precession as the animal moves through a place field #cite(<okeefePhaseRelationshiphippocampal1993>). Abolishing theta oscillations produces impairment in spatial memory #cite(<winsonLosshippocampalTheta1978>), and theta features heavily in many models of place cell activity (REF). In primates, human and non-human, theta oscillations are also functionally related to movement or navigational search (Jacobs et al 2010) but differ from rodents in both consistency and frequency band. Firstly, primate theta oscillations are non-continuous and occur in short bouts of 2.75 cycles (Watrous et al 2011), suggesting that among the inputs that primate place cells integrate, velocity information and theta phase-locking may have less, and other sensory inputs more, weight. Secondly, while rodent theta frequencies have the same lower bound for virtual and real-world navigation (Chen et al 2018), there is mixed evidence in primates. Some studies have found on average stratification of primate theta oscillations into two frequency ranges for virtual (1 to 4Hz) vs real-world (5 to 10Hz) navigation (Jacobs et al 2007; Courellis et al 2019; REF MONKEYS), but there is as yet no understanding of how different theta frequency bands influence spatial cell activity. Additionally, one study comparing real and virtual navigation within subject showed no difference in theta frequency (Bohbot et al 2017). Our specific aim 1 will resolve the discrepancy by comparing theta frequencies in the same animal under different navigational demands and assess the dependence of different spatial cells on discontinuous theta waves. */

Another important difference between rodent and primate spatial cognition was discovered in the form of spatial view cells in the primate hippocampus #cite(<rollsSpatialViewCells1997>) #cite(<rollsSpatialViewCells1999>), selective for the location in visual space being viewed. To date, this cell type has not been seen in the rodent, but one study found evidence of these cells in a species of food caching bird #cite(<payneNeuralRepresentationsSpace2021>). In the study with chaired NHPs, the authors were able show instances of a cell firing to views of a specific part of the environment from various locations and orientations, suggesting independence of spatial view from place or head direction coding. However, they found no evidence of place cells and could not compare the coding of location in visual versus occupied space. A later study in virtual reality found spatial view and place responses in the same ensemble, and even in the same cell #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>). However, in this study, place and gaze were correlated by the restrictions of the experimental setup, so the contribution of place cells to spatial view responses and vice versa could not be completely ruled out (Wirth, personal communication). 

Visual landmarks are known to be important in anchoring place, head direction, and grid cells to the environmental frame of reference in rodents, but the discovery of spatial view cells suggests additional importance of visual inputs to spatial cognition in primates. Compared to rodents, primates have much greater visual acuity, more binocular vision, and the ability to foveate #cite(<wirthPlaceViewFirstperson2023>). Behaviorally, they also spend much more time in visual exploration of the environment, and spatial view cells may provide a basis for building a map of space prior to occupying it. Additionally, spatial view cell activity may support mental translocations, such as imagining oneself in a different location, by triggering place cell activity to an unoccupied and inaccessible location with the activation of spatial view activity related to that location. 

