#figure(image("figures/figure1.png"),
   caption:[A) Schematic of the monkey performing the virtual navigation task while sitting in an enclosed primate chair with an opening for the right arm to operate a joystick. A drop of fruit juice was delivered through a mouthpiece when the target poster (in this case the cat) was reached within 25 seconds. Neural activity and eye movements were recorded throughout the experiment. B) Top-down view of the virtual arena showing the location of each of the 6 posters. C) Distribution of performance for each of the 6 posters in each of the 61 recording sessions. *The black bars represent ...* D) Path length between pairs of posters relative to the optimal path length. E) Sample trajectories from one session. The green dots indicate the starting position and the red dot the ending position. F) A 3D rendering of the chamber (red) and the microdrive (blue) used to position and advance 124 individual electrodes (teal) down into the hippocampus. ]) <fig_task_overview>
   
We recorded from the dorsal aspect of the hippocampus while a single non-human primate (NHP) subject performed a virtual navigation task. The task was loosely modeled on the virtual taxi task from #cite(<ekstromCellularNetworksUnderlying2003>, form:"prose"). @fig_task_overview shows a schematic of the task. The animal was seated in a primate chair in front of a computer monitor, and navigated a virtual maze using a joystick. The maze consisted of four pillars equally spaced in a square room ($25 times 25$ virtual units). Some of the pillars had posters, which served as navigation targets. We used a total of 6 different posters. At the beginning of each trial, the animal was shown a picture of one of the 6 posters, which represented the location in the maze to which the animal was required to navigate. If within 25 seconds, the subject made their way to the target with a distance of less than 2 virtual units, and faced the target with an angle of less than 60 degrees, a drop of juice was automatically delivered through a mouth piece. The animal performed a total of 61 recording sessions, where each session typically consisted of around 400 trials, with an average performance of 96% (@fig_task_overview C). @fig_task_overview D shows all trajectories between each pair of posters for one session, highlighting how the animal appeared to favor the shortest route, and suggesting that it was indeed navigating using an internal spatial map of the arena. To quantify this, we computed the shortest path between posters using a graph representation of the maze floor. That is, we divided the floor of the arena into $10 times 10$ bins, and used the adjacency relation between bins to compute the shortest path between poster locations. We then computed the path taken by the animal in each trial, and divided the length of each path by the shortest path length. @fig_task_overview D shows the average of this ratio for each pair of posters across all 61 recording sessions. For most pairs, the ratio was close to one, indicating an optimal path. The largest deviation from optimality occurred for trajectories from the rabbit to the cat poster, and from the donkey poster to the rabbit poster. For the former pair, they animal sometimes took a detour, as can be seen in the first row, second column in @fig_task_overview E. Interestingly, for the session shown in E, the animal did not take detours going in the other direction, from the cat to the rabbit poster (second row, fifth column in @fig_task_overview E). Interestingly, we found that path length was significantly influenced by the head direction of the animal at the start of the trial for 20 out of 30 unique poster combinations (@fig_s1_path_length_vs_head_direction). We found that the strongest such influence ($r^2 = 0.39$) again occurred for paths between the rabbit and the cat poster. These results suggest that the animal's behavior was guided by an internal map of the arena, and that paths were chosen based on optimal path traveled along the current head direction.


== Neural place selectivity
Across the 61 recording sessions, we isolated 364 cells from the hippocampus. The median cell count per session was 5, the 5th percentile was 2, and the 90th percentile was 13. 

We first identified cells that were significantly modulated by the animal's spatial location in the arena. To do this, we followed the approach standardized in the rodent literature #cite(<skaggsThetaPhasePrecession1996>). Briefly, we binned the floor of the arena into $40 times 40$ bins and counted the number of spikes falling within each spatial bin across all trials in a recording session. We used the Spatial Information Criterion (SIC) #cite(<skaggsThetaPhasePrecession1996>) to determine whether the spatial distribution of these spikes conveyed information about where the animal was in the arena; a cell whose spikes mostly occurred in one part of the arena would be associated with a high SIC, while a cell whose spikes were scattered uniformly around the arena would have a low SIC. Prior to computing the SIC, we smoothed both the spike counts and the occupancy in each spatial bin using a diffusion based smoothing technique #cite(<coifmanDiffusionMaps2006>), and obtained an estimate of the firing rate by dividing the smoothed spike counts by the smoothed occupancy. We compared the SIC of the actual spike trains to that of surrogates obtained by randomly circularly shifting the spike times in the spike trains with respect to the position of the animal. 
For cells with significantly higher SIC than the surrogates, we identified place fields by searching for contiguous groups of bins in which the firing rate exceeded the firing rate of the surrogates. We used a cluster-based analysis to identify these regions, and only groups of bins that occurred in less than 0.1% of surrogates, obtained by randomly distributing the significant bins, were identified as place fields #cite(<gulliContextdependentRepresentationsObjects2020>). 

We found a total of 177 of the 364 (48.6%) recorded cells to be spatially selective. @fig_place_field_summary A shows 3 examples of spatially selective cells. The first cell (left) exhibited significantly higher SIC than the surrogates (B, left), but no contiguous regions larger than those expected by chance were identified. In contrast, cells shown in the middle and right exhibited SIC that were significantly above chance, and where the spatial distribution of firing was more concentrated than expected by chance. We identified such cells as place cells. For example, the cell in the middle exhibited one strong place field in the north-south oriented corridor of the arena, near the red pillar. The cell on the right is a representative of a population of cells with more than one place field, with the fields distributed around the arena. Out of the 177 spatially selective cells, 121 (68.4%) were identified as place cells by this contiguity criteria (@fig_place_field_summary C). We found a substantial portion (53.7%;65/121) of place cells with more than one place field (@fig_place_field_summary C). 

#figure(image("figures/figure2.png"),
       caption: [Summary of place fields across 177 cells with significant place activity. A) 3 examples of cells with place field, with the SIC scores for each cell shown in B). The box plots represent the distribution of SIC scores for the randomly shifted surrogates and the dotted vertical line represents the SIC score of the maps shown above. The black outlines in A represent the individual place fields. C) The distribution of the number of response fields per cell, with place selective cells with place fields shown in gray and those without place fields shown in black. D) The distribution of field size, in units of virtual units square. E) Distribution of peak firing rate for cells with and without place fields (shown in gray and black, respectively). F) The number of overlapping place fields in each of the spatial bins in the maze. ]) <fig_place_field_summary>


We also ran a simpler algorithm for identifying place fields that is more similar to what has been used in the rodent literature. Briefly, we first identified peaks in the firing rate map, defined as bins that exceeded the $99.9^"th"$ percentile of the activity across bins, and then grew a region around that peak to encompass activity larger than or equal to half of the distance from the peak to the background activity, defined as the $10^"th"$ percentile of the firing across all spatial bins. This approach resulted in more place fields per place selective cell than the more conservative approach (@fig_s4_alt_place_field_def), with 119 out 121 place cells having more than one place field.

