=== View selective neural responses
We estimated the region of the visual space that each cell responded to by first transforming the 2D pixel coordinates recorded by the eye tracker to 3D gaze coordinates in the virtual world. This was done via Unity 3D's ray casting functionality. Briefly, a ray was cast from the 2D pixel coordinates (on the virtual camera plane) through the virtual camera lens and into the 3D world. The point at which that ray intersected an object, e.g. the floor, the ceiling, the wall, or a pillar, was the estimated gaze coordinate associated with that eye position. We binned these virtual gaze coordinates using a larger bin size compared to what we used for the floor, resulting in approximately the same number of view bins as we had place bins (1312 view bins compared to 1344 place bin). For each bin, we computed the firing rate of a cell by dividing the total number spikes occurring in that bin by the total amount of time that the gaze was in that bin. 
To estimate a cell's view fields, we followed the same procedure as when estimating the place fields. A cell's overall view selectivity was estimated via the SIC, and view fields were identified by finding groups of contiguous view bins with activity that exceeded that of the surrogates. 

@fig_view_field_summary shows 3 examples of view selective cells (A and B), 2 of which had identifiable view fields. 
Across the population of recorded cells, 192 (52.7%) were identified as view selective, using the same SIC criterion as for spatially selective cells. Of these, 117 (60.9%) had at least one identified view field and were thus classified as view cells. The majority (80/117; 68.4%) of view cells had either 1 or 2 identified view fields (@fig_view_field_summary C). Similar to what we found for place cells, the mean firing rate of view selective cells with at least one view field was significantly higher than for view selective cells without fields (Kruskal-Wallis test, p=0.0002, @fig_view_field_summary E). The median of the former distribution was 10.1 ± *??* Hz, while for the latter, it was 6.3 ± *??* Hz.   

The identified view fields were mainly distributed on the walls, floor, and the pillars (@fig_view_field_summary F). We found that most of the view fields on the floor were concentrated near the poster locations (*numbers for this*). View fields on the walls were not distributed equally, with the east wall having the most number of view fields (34), followed by the north wall (24), the south wall (22) and the west wall (8). View fields on the pillars tended to overlap with the posters. In fact, 10 of the 12 view fields that overlapped with the green pillar also overlapped with the crocodile poster, 9 of 20 view fields on the red poster overlapped with the donkey, and 11 with the pig, 2 out of 7 view fields on the yellow pillar overlapped with the cat poster, 8 out of 10 view fields on the blue poster overlapped with the rabbit, and 3 out of 10 with the camel poster. 
For the 44/80 (55%) view cells for which at least one view field overlapped with a poster, 11/44 (25%) exhibited a significant difference between when the overlapping poster was a goal in the current trial compared to when it was not (2-sided permutation test, p < 0.025) #cite(<ekstromCellularNetworksUnderlying2003>). Of these 11 cells, 7 (63.6%) had lower activity when the poster was a goal than when it was not, while the rest had higher activity. Two sample cells are shown in @fig_s5_poster_goal_selectivity, one with higher firing rate when the its preferred poster was the goal and one with lower firing rate. 

We were curious whether the cells with view fields overlapping at least one poster location were simply poster identity cells, i.e. they responded to the image of the poster simply as a visual input, rather than as an object embedded in a 3D scene. To investigate this, we compared the activity of these cells during the cue presentation period, when a flat image of the goal poster filled most of the screen. A cell that was simply visually selective to a particular poster would be expected to have similar tuning during the navigation period as during the cue presentation period (a cell selective to the crocodile poster during cue presentation, for instance, would also respond preferentially to the crocodile poster whenever it was visible during the navigation phase). 
We determined the preferred posters of a cell during the cue presentation period by a GLM-based hierarchical fitting procedure that grouped posters into preferred and non-preferred sets (see Methods). We then compared the set of preferred posters during cue presentation to the posters overlapping with the cell's view fields. At the extreme, a pure visual input cell would only have view fields overlapping with posters preferred during the cue presentation. 
We found that a total of 45 of the 85 view cells (52.9%) where the view fields intersected with at least one poster also responded preferentially to at least one of those posters during the cue period. None of the cells had exactly overlapping poster preference between the two periods. Five cells had 3 posters in common, 24 cells had 2 posters in common and 16 cells had 1 poster in common, while the remaining 40 cells had no posters in common between its preference during cue and navigation. Since these cells appear to encode the poster as a navigational landmark, we refer to them as landmark cells.

Together, these results suggest that cells in the hippocampus represent views that inform routes between landmarks.


