 ==== Place field directionality

 The interpretation of place fields as representing purely an encoding of spatial location has long been the subject of nuance. Since the spatial responses are collected while animals are actively navigating, a natural question is whether the direction of movement influences those responses. For a purely spatial field, the responses should be the same regardless of what direction the field is being traversed, since it only encodes spatial position. However, experiments in rodents #cite(<mullerDirectionalFiringProperties1994>) as well as non-human primates #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>) #cite(<maoSpatialModulationHippocampal2021>) have found that a substantial portion of cells with identified place fields also exhibit directional modulation. 
 To estimate the degree to which spatial cells in this study exhibited such directional modulation, we looked at responses within the place field when the animal traversed the field in the direction of its major axis, and compare those to responses in the opposite direction. Briefly, a field's major axis was computed by performing a singular value decomposition (SVD) on the point cloud representing spatial positions falling with the field, and then identifying the singular vector associated with the largest singular value as the major axis. 
 @fig_directed_fields A-D shows an example of a single place cell where both of its identified place fields showed a strong directionality. Activity in the field in purple outline, which was near the crocodile poster on the red pillar, was significantly higher (purple dot vs distribution in B) when the animal traversed the field from west to east (orange arrow in A) than in the opposite direction. Conversely, the activity in the field with orange outline, which was near the pig poster on the blue pillar, was significantly higher (orange dot in vs distribution in D) when the animal traversed the field from east to west (black arrow in C). 
 


