         F) Number of place cells with with significant place field directionality (purple) compared to the number of place cells (gray). G) The distribution of distance from the nearest poster to the place field center for non-directional (gray) and directional (purple) place fields. ]) <fig_directed_fields>
         
         
 Overall, we found that 37 out of the 121 place cells (30.6%) had at least one field with a strong directional preference. These directional place fields were mostly located in the center of the arena (@fig_directed_fields E), and mostly along the east-west corridor. The fields were also concentrated near the locations of the six posters, which mirrors the distribution of place fields overall (@fig_place_field_summary F). A Mann-Whitney U-test on the distribution of Euclidean distances from each place field center to the nearest poster confirmed that the two distributions were not significantly different (p=0.07, @fig_directed_fields G). /*Can we make a case that directionality is not directly driven by behaviour near the posters? */ This suggested that the directionality of the place fields was not primarily driven by stereotypical behavior near the posters, which would favor movement either toward or away from them, at least when they served as the current (towards) or previous (away) navigation goal. 


=== View field activity for directional place fields
It is possible that some of the apparent view selectivity we observed could be explained by a cell having a directional place field. For instance, a cell with a place field just north of the intersection between the yellow and the red pillar, and where the cell was most active when that place field was traversed from west to east, would also tend to favor a view field on the eastern wall, since the gaze would naturally fall on that wall for that particular direction of traversal. 
To establish whether the firing rate within a view field could be explained by the directionality of the place field, we computed the firing rate for each view bin for trials in which the subject was in the place field, traversing it along the preferred direction of the field. We then computed the degree of occlusion between each position in the place field and each gaze location in the view field. If at least 50% of the view field was visible from the place field, with a line-of-sight deviation from the preferred direction of traversal of less than $60 degree$, we identified the view responses of this cell as at least partially explainable by a combination of spatial selectivity and directional preference. 

Among the 35 view cells that also had directional place fields, we found 15 cells (42.9%) where the activity within at least one view field was compatible with a combination of spatial selectivity and directional preference. Out of these 15 cells, we found 9 (66.7%) in which the single compatible view field overlapped with a poster. In one cell, one out of two compatible view fields overlapped with a poster, and for the remaining 5 cells, there was no overlap between posters and view fields. 
Since the posters constituted a clear visual landmark, both for their inherent saliency, and their role as potential navigation targets, we deemed it unlikely that the view responses to the posters was a result of purely spatial activity. The conclusion, then, was that out of the 117 cells with view fields, only 5 (4.3%) could reasonably be explained as arising from purely spatial activity. /*Can we we also look at the distance from the corresponding place fields to the posters? Anectdotally, some of the place fields are right next to the posters. Also, I think we need at least a supplementary figure for this analysis.*/

