=== Mixed selectivity to place and view
A distinguishing feature of primate hippocampal cells compared to those of rodents is the relative higher incidence of mixed selectivity, i.e. selectivity to more than one experimental variable #cite(<maoSpatialModulationHippocampal2021>) #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>) #cite(<yanDivergentNeuralRepresentations2026>). Here, we focused on mixed selectivity between spatial location and gaze position. We first computed the number of cells that were selective to both place and view, using the SIC as a selectivity criteria, and found 158 cells that were selective to both. This number constituted 89.3% of the spatially selective cells and 82.3% of the view selective cells, showing that majority of cells were mixed selective for both place and view #cite(<maoSpatialModulationHippocampal2021>).

In the subsequent analyses, we focused on a subset of 89 cells for which we could identify at least one place field and one view field. An example of such a cell is shown in @fig_place_view_conjunction_example. This particular cell had 1 view field (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example A, left), located in the north-east corner of the maze, near the donkey poster. In addition, the cell had two place fields (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example A, right); one between the yellow and the red pillar (red dots), and one near the pig poster (on the blue pillar; yellow dots). To determine whether the activity of this cell was driven, at least in part, by the a specific combination of spatial location and gaze, we examined the place and view fields when conditioning on either a specific view field, or a specific place field. If the cell responded to place and view independently, we would not expect to find any difference between the unconditioned and conditioned place and view map. On the other hand, if the activity of the call was driven by a specific combination of place and view, then the unconditional and conditional maps could differ. To investigate this in detail, we first looked at a cell's view map when we only included time bins in which the animal was inside the cell's place field. An example of this is shown in @fig_place_view_conjunction_example B. The top panel shows the view field (after smoothing) for bins where the position fell within the cell's place field, shown in red in the bottom panel. When comparing with the original view map (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example A), we see that the structures of the two maps are similar; both show elevated activity near the north-east corner. To test whether this structure was linked to the position being within the place field, we first confirmed that the firing rate within the view field was higher than outside the view field, even after conditioning on the place field. The distribution of mean firing rates in these non-view-field regions is represented in blue in @fig_place_view_conjunction_example C, while the mean firing rate within the view field is represented by the red diamond. The latter was higher than the $99.9^"th"$ percentile of the former, showing that the view field represented a region of elevated firing, also in the conditional map. To test whether the conditional view map was specific to the place field, we compared the mean firing rate within the view field in the conditional map to the distribution of mean firing rates within the view field when we conditioned other positions around the arena. Specifically, we created conditional view maps where we conditioned on place bins #emph[not] part of the place field of the cell, but from where the view field could still be seen. The bins fulfilling this criteria for the cell in @fig_place_view_conjunction_example are plotted in dark gray in the bottom panel @fig_place_view_conjunction_example B. When we compared this distribution of mean firing rates for the non-place field conditioned view maps to the mean of the place field conditioned view map, we found the latter was above the $99.9^"th"$ percentile of the former (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example C, gray distribution vs red asterisk). 
This shows that the activity within the view field was higher when the animal was in a location covered by the place field, compared to other positions not part of the place field, and suggests that the observed view field represented a conjunctive coding of place and view.

We ran a similar analysis comparing the place map conditioned on the view field to the unconditioned place map, and also here found that the place field was specific to the gaze of the animal falling within the view field. (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example, D-E). 

#figure(image("figures/conjunction_figure_new.png"),
    caption: [Example of a cell with a significant conjunction between its place and view response. A) Left) The 1 view field of the cell, indicated by the red squares. Right) The 2 place fields, also indicted by colored dots. B) View responses conditioned on the red place field (between the yellow and red pillars;bottom panel). C) The mean firing rate within the red view field when the  animal's position was within the red place field (red asterisk), compared to the distribution of mean firing rates in the view field when the animal occupied one of of the gray bins (gray distribution). The blue distribution represents the average firing rate outside the view field, while the red diamond represents the mean within the field, after conditioning on the place field. The green dot represents the mean within the view field in the original view map (A).  D-E similar as B-C, but focusing on the place responses. The spatial map in middle panel shows the average firing rate in each spatial bin when the animal's gaze fell within the purple view field. F) Three cells with a large distance between place and view field. The view fields are shown as red tiles while the position of the place fields is indicated vertical lines.]) <fig_place_view_conjunction_example>
    
We repeated the above analyses for all 89 cells with both place and view fields and found that 63 of them (70.8%) had at least one place-view field combination consistent with conjunctive coding. The majority (48/89;53.9%) of these exhibited conjunctions for either 1 (23 cells) or 2 (25 cells) combinations of place and view fields (see @fig_s6_conjunctive_cells for more details). 

The poster locations provided natural anchor points as the subject navigated the maze. Since standing in front of a poster typically involved looking at the poster, cells that responded to the poster could be identified as conjunctive in the above analysis, since their responses would be modulated both by what the subject was looking at (i.e. the poster), and where he was looking from (i.e. in front of poster). Thus, we computed the fraction of conjunctive cells where at least one of the conjunctive view fields (i.e. a view field that was part of a conjunction with a place field) overlapped with a poster. The majority (56/63; 88.9%) of place-view conjunctive cells had at least one place-view conjunction where the view field overlapped with a poster, where 52 cells (82.3%) had a poster in all of the view fields of their place-view conjunctions. Furthermore, 37 (58.7%) of the place-view conjunctive cells were also landmark cells, i.e. with similar poster preference during the cue and navigation periods, and for 35 of these, the poster overlap of the view fields of the place-view conjunctions corresponded exactly to the preferred poster during the cue period. In other words, a little over half of the conjunctive cells (35/63;55.6%) appeared to exhibit conjunctions that were exclusively driven by their role as landmark cells.

We observed two types of landmark conjunctive responses. One type occurred when the place field was located right next to the poster, i.e. where the cell appeared to encode the fact that the subject was standing next to a poster. However, we also observed a small subset of cells where the distance between the center of the place field and the view field was larger. In particular, we found 3 cells (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example F) for which the distance between the place field and the view was was larger than 7.5 virtual units. Two of these cells had a place field near the green pillar and a view field on the blue pillar (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example F left and middle), near the cat poster. The 3rd cell had a place field between the red and the green pillar, and a view field on the yellow pillar, near the camel poster (@fig_place_view_conjunction_example F, right).
/*The index of these cells in the place-view conjugate list is  44, 53
, 62*/
The remaining 7 cells, or 7.9% of the cells with both place and view fields, had conjunctions that did not directly involve the posters, but rather encoded the view of particular pillars and walls from specific places around the maze, and could represent location-stamped view points that could aid in the subject's navigation, akin to the viewpoint cells mentioned in #cite(<wirthPlaceViewFirstperson2023>).

