@fig_overall_summary shows a summary of the different classes of cell we identified and the interactions between them. In addition to the points made above, it is worth noting that only 2 cells with directional place field were not also conjunctive with view, and that only 3 cells with poster selectivity during the navigation period were not also conjunctive with place. In other words, both poster selectivity and place field directionality were primarily expressed in cells with conjunctive responses to both place and view.

== Discussion
We estimated the place and view selectivity in cells recorded from the hippocampus of an NHP subject navigating a virtual maze. We found that 48.6% of the cells had significant spatial selectivity, and 52.7% had significant view selectivity. We identified a subset of these cells as either place or view cells by requiring groups of contiguous bins to have significantly higher activity than randomly shifted surrogates, resulting in 68.4% of spatially selective cells being identified as place cells, and 60.9% of view selective cells being identified as view cells. A subset (30.6%) of place cells had place fields that were significantly modulated by the direction at which the fields were traversed. Moreover, a subset of the view cells (72.6%) had view fields that overlapped with the location of the target posters.
In addition, we found that 24.5% of cells had both place and view fields, of which 70.8% encoded information about place and view in a non-linear manner.

The extent of spatial encoding found in the primate hippocampus covers a wide range. On the lower end of the spectrum we find studies involving freely moving animals engaged in foraging #cite(<maoSpatialModulationHippocampal2021>). Here, the researches found that the animals' position in the real world best explained the activity of only 5% of the cells in the hippocampus. In a follow-up study, where they compared the activity in the hippocampus between real and virtual navigation, #cite(<yanDivergentNeuralRepresentations2026>, form: "prose") found a substantial increase in the fraction of hippcampal cells with selectivity to position in real-world compared to virtual navigation. The proportion of cells with spatial selectivity was still relatively low, however, at around 17%. 
#cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>, form: "prose") also recorded from the hippocampus of monkeys preforming a virtual navigation task, and found that 59% of responsive cells (activity > 0.01 Hz) had significant SIC. At the higher end of the spectrum, in a similar study by  #cite(<gulliContextdependentRepresentationsObjects2020>, form: "prose"), 76.0% of hippocampal cells showed significant SIC. In this study, in addition to an associative memory task, where the monkeys navigated to learned targets in changing contexts, the authors also included a foraging task. In this task, the proportion of spatially selective cells decreased substantially to 27.3%.
/*Also potentially include Ludvig et al (2004) and Courellis et al (2019), though the latter uses marmosets*/

Our results are closer to those of #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>, form:"prose"), despite our task structure being qualitatively different from theirs. Our subject navigated freely, only being prevented from using the joystick to reverse his movement. In particular, though it was possible that he used each of the pillars as landmarks, similar to the landmarks in #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>, form:"prose"), we did not require him to start from the center of the maze at each trial. Rather, our task was continuous, requiring the subject to find his way to next poster from the poster that served as a target in the previous trial. We also did not make any attempt to restrict the subject's movement in the maze, though the actual chosen trajectories between targets were close to optimal (@fig_task_overview). 

One possible explanation for the differing proportions of place selective cells among these studies is tied to differences in the details of how the different tasks were designed. A clear example of how changes in task elicit changes in spatial coding properties in hippocampal cells is found in #cite(<yanDivergentNeuralRepresentations2026>, form: "prose"), where the authors explicitly set out to test whether the overall context (real-world vs virtual navigation) affected the degree to which the same population of hippcampal cells represented space (as well as other aspects of the task). They found that the proportion of cells representing allocentric coding significantly increased in real-world vs virtual navigation, which at the very least supports the idea that overall context matters for the hippocampal spatial code. Interestingly, the authors also found that the degree of egocentric coding was higher in the virtual compared to the real-world navigation task. The virtual navigation task was similar to ours in that subjects freely navigated to find hidden reward locations. In our task, the presence of pillars introduced constraints on what paths the subject could take, which could have affected the way in which space was encoded by the hippocampus. At the abstract level, these kinds of constraints could result in the hippocampus needing to maintain a more complicated code of where things are. In the task used by #cite(<gulliContextdependentRepresentationsObjects2020>), the animals needed to encode a contextual cue to make the correct choice at the end of the Y-maze, potentially creating a more complicated code that required more place cells to maintain. This is consistent with their observation that a random foraging task in the same arena resulted in a substantial reduction in the fraction of place selective cells. On the other hand, the task in #cite(<yanDivergentNeuralRepresentations2026>, form: "prose") took place in an open arena where the hidden target locations could be found using spatial view only, which is consistent with the much larger fraction of cells encoding spatial view compared to position reported by the authors. 

The large fraction of cells coding for spatial view is consistent with a key observation in our study, i.e. that the majority (89.3%) of cells with place selective responses was also significantly modulated by the gaze position of the subject. This high degree of mixed selectivity is consistent with what most previous studies have found #cite(<furuyaPlacerelatedNeuronalActivity2014>) #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>) #cite(<gulliContextdependentRepresentationsObjects2020>) #cite(<yanDivergentNeuralRepresentations2026>), and different from what most studies in rodents have shown in similar tasks (but see #cite(<ormondHippocampalPlaceCells2022>, form: "prose")). The lack of spatial view cells in rodents is often attributed to the fact that they rely far less on vision for navigation compared to e.g. primates.  Interestingly, one study in a species of food caching bird, a highly visual animal, also found a high fraction of mixed place and gaze selectivity #cite(<payneNeuralRepresentationsSpace2021>), similar to the results in primates. The consensus emerging, then, is that species with a strong emphasis on vision tend to exhibit hippocampal activity that depends strongly on both where the animal is and what it is looking at #cite(<wirthPlaceViewFirstperson2023>). 
Supporting this view, we found a subset of cells with view fields near posters that also modulated the activity within that field based on the location of the animal in the maze. In fact, most of the mixed selective cells that we analyzed exhibited a conjunction between place and view that was tied to the location of posters. Since the posters were the main landmarks available to the subject, in addition to the pillars, these cells could serve a similar role the landmark cells in #cite(<wirthGazeinformedTasksituatedRepresentation2017>)
These kinds of location-stamped view snapshots could act as the components of an integrated visual path, representing what the view should look like if the subject is on the right path towards the goal. 


== Methods <methods>

=== Subjects and Surgical Procedures
One male, adult macaque (Macaca fascicularis) was used in the experiments. An MRI scan of the animal’s brain was performed to identify the hippocampus (shown in @fig_task_overview, and is approximately 20 to 40 mm from the surface of the brain) in order to position a customized designed titanium chamber precisely so that 124 glass-insulated tungsten microelectrodes loaded into the chamber could be lowered into the hippocampus. 

=== Behavioral Tasks
In order to understand the role of the hippocampus in virtual navigation, we trained an NHP to sit in front of a computer monitor and navigate through a virtual environment using a joystick. The environment was created in Unity3D, and consisted of a square arena with 4 pillars located in each of the four quadrants. The animal needed to navigate to posters located on those pillars. An example of the arena layout is shown @fig_task_overview. The joystick movements, the location of the animal in the virtual environment, the eye positions on the screen, and the neural signals were be recorded while the animal performed the task.

The 3D gaze position in the virtual environment was obtained through Unity3D's raycasting functionality, which makes use of the virtual camera configuration to compute which 3D world position a 2D screen coordinate corresponds to.

=== Neural recording

We  recorded from the hippocampus using a micro-drive recording system from Gray Matter Research (Montana, USA). The system is shown in @fig_task_overview. The system was custom designed to target the hippocampal formation in the animal (identified using an MRI scan), which allowed us to precisely position the chamber during implantation. Using the MRI scan, we also measured the depth of the hippocampus from the top of the skull for each electrode, and lowered each electrode to the prescribed depth.

We recorded broadband neuronal activity while the animal performed the task. The high-frequency components (500 Hz to 7.5 kHz) were used to perform spike sorting to identify action potentials from different neurons, using Mountainsort #cite(<chungFullyAutomatedApproach2017>). 

=== Neural analysis 
#emph[Spatial selectivity]. In order to understand the relationship between cell firing and location, we computed the spatial information content (SIC) #cite(<skaggsThetaPhasePrecession1996>) for each cell. We first assigned the spikes of a cell to a spatial bin, and computed the total number of spikes, as well as the total occupancy for each bin. Prior to computing the SIC, we smoothed the spike counts and occupancy separately using a Laplace kernel. This kernel was constructed using the connectivity matrix $A_(i j)$, where $A_(i j)$ was 1 if bin $i$ and bin $j$ were connected and 0 otherwise. The symmetrically normalized Laplace matrix $L_s$ was then given by

$ L_s =  I - D^(-1/2) A D^(-1/2) $

The smoothed spike counts $X_s$ and the smoothed occupancy $W_s$ were then given by

$ X_s = (I - alpha L_s)^n X \
  W_s = (I - alpha L_s)^n W 
$,

where $n$ is the number of iterations, typically 25 or 50, and the smoothed firing rate in bin $k$ was $lambda_k = X_(s,k)/W_(s,k)$.

To prevent bins with an inflated firing rate due to very low occupancy from distorting the maps, we required that a bin was occupied for at least 50ms for least 5 trials to be included in the analysis.
In order to assess the significance of the SIC, we computed the SIC for 10,000 shuffled spike trains where the original spike times were circularly shifted by a random amount chosen uniformly between 0.1 and 0.9 times the total recording time. An SIC threshold was then computed based on the $99.9^"th"$ percentile of the shuffled distribution. /*The intra-session stability for each cell was also calculated as the correlation between the first and second halves of each session. */

#emph[Place field direction selectivity]. For each identified spatial field, we estimated the direction selectivity by first identifying the major axis of the field. This was done by first computing the singular value decomposition (SVD) of the points falling within the spatial field and using the first singular vector as an estimate of the field's major axis. We then identified trials in which the spatial field was traversed either along or opposite the major axis. We computed the firing rates for each set of trials, and used a permutation test to check wether the firing rate was significantly different between the two sets. Since the number of trials could be quite different for the two sets, we used a variant of the permutation test were we randomly swapped firing rates between the two sets, and then computed the distribution of the difference of mean firing rates for 10,000 such surrogate sets. 


#emph[View Cells.] The gaze positions returned by the ray-casting procedure was used to obtain the mean firing rates at each gaze position. To establish whether the activity of a cell was significantly influenced by gaze position, we followed a similar procedure as for place selectivity. The raw firing rate maps were smoothed with the same algorithm, except now with a Laplacian matrix obtained by computing the adjacency between bins spanning the full 3D layout of the arena (i.e. walls, pillars and ceiling, in addition to the floor). Because gaze is inherently more transient than position, we only required a gaze bin to be occupied for at least 20ms for at least 5 trials, as opposed to 50 ms for place (see above). 
Similar to place selectivity, we classified a cell as view selective if the SIC of the smoothed firing rate maps exceeded the $99.9^"th"$ percentile of the randomly time-shifted surrogates.
In order to qualify as spatial view cells, we conditioned on the peak location of the view map to analyze the place responses. Cells needed to exhibit more than one peak in the view-conditioned place map to qualify as a spatial view cell. 

#emph[Place-conditioned view responses.] It was possible that spatial view cells were a subset of cells that exhibit mixed selectivity between place selectivity and view selectivity. In other to disambiguate these two possibilities, we performed a two-way ANOVA analysis to identify neurons that exhibited main effects of place and view, as well as those that exhibited significant interactions between the two factors. 

#emph[Poster selectivity during the cue period]. We determined the poster selectivity during the cue presentation period by fitting a GLM to the total number of spikes emitted by a cell during that period, with the poster IDs (1-6) as categorical inputs and a Poisson distribution as the outcome distribution. We determined significance by fitting the same GLM to surrogate data where the relationship between poster ID and spike counts was scrambled. The fit was considered significant if the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) of the data was below the 1st percentile of that of the surrogates. To determine which posters a cell was selective to, we successively grouped posters with the highest fitted coefficients together and then refit a GLM with only two groups; one group consisting of the posters with the $k$ most preferred posters, and another group with the remaining posters. We increased $k$ until the GLM fit was no longer significant, and identified the top $k$ posters as the ones the cell was selective to.  

